---
import DsLayout from "../../layouts/DsLayout.astro";
import DoDont from "../../components/ds/DoDont.astro";
---

<DsLayout
  section="Guidelines"
  title="Content"
  lead="How the site sounds, and the small rules that keep every page sounding like the same person wrote it."
>
  <section aria-labelledby="voice">
    <h2 id="voice">Voice</h2>
    <p class="ds-text">One designer talking to one person. The voice holds steady; the tone shifts with the page.</p>
    <div class="ds-grid">
      <div class="ds-card">
        <span class="ds-card-title">Plain</span>
        <span class="ds-card-body">Say it the way you'd say it across a table. "I design and build websites," not "I deliver end-to-end digital solutions."</span>
      </div>
      <div class="ds-card">
        <span class="ds-card-title">Specific</span>
        <span class="ds-card-body">Numbers, names, and outcomes over adjectives. "131 primitive variables" beats "a robust token library."</span>
      </div>
      <div class="ds-card">
        <span class="ds-card-title">First person</span>
        <span class="ds-card-body">"I" for Ryan, "you" for the reader. "We" only when a team really did the work, and then name the team.</span>
      </div>
      <div class="ds-card">
        <span class="ds-card-title">Confident, not loud</span>
        <span class="ds-card-body">State what happened. Skip "incredibly," "cutting-edge," and exclamation points. Let the work make the claim.</span>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="tone">
    <h2 id="tone">Tone by context</h2>
    <div class="ds-table-wrap">
      <table class="ds-table">
        <thead><tr><th scope="col">Where</th><th scope="col">Tone</th><th scope="col">Example</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>Homepage, About</td><td>Direct, a little dry</td><td class="muted">"Designing systems, sites and brands."</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Case studies</td><td>Specific, credits others, shows the hard part</td><td class="muted">"Four of us did this work."</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Forms</td><td>Warm, low pressure, explains why</td><td class="muted">"Helps me suggest the right scope. No wrong answers."</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Errors</td><td>Calm, says what to do next</td><td class="muted">"Something went wrong. Please try again or email me directly."</td></tr>
          <tr><td>404, Easter eggs</td><td>Playful is fine here</td><td class="muted">"Cmd + I to peek under the hood."</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="mechanics">
    <h2 id="mechanics">Mechanics</h2>
    <div class="ds-table-wrap">
      <table class="ds-table">
        <thead><tr><th scope="col">Topic</th><th scope="col">Rule</th><th scope="col">Example</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>Capitalization</td><td>Sentence case for everything: headings, buttons, labels, page titles. Proper nouns keep their caps.</td><td class="mono">Start a website project</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Dashes</td><td>No em dashes. Use a comma, a colon, a period, or parentheses instead. Use an en dash for ranges.</td><td class="mono">Apr 2025 – Present</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Numbers</td><td>Numerals for all numbers, including one through nine, because readers scan for them.</td><td class="mono">3 brands, 6 modes</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Money</td><td>Round, with "k" for thousands, and "to" for ranges in prose.</td><td class="mono">$3k to $7k</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Dates</td><td>Month abbreviated, no day unless it matters.</td><td class="mono">Oct 2026</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Lists</td><td>Serial comma. No period on fragments; a period on full sentences.</td><td class="mono">tokens, components, and docs</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Contractions</td><td>Use them. They're how people talk.</td><td class="mono">I'll, you're, don't</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Arrows</td><td>→ moves you forward on this site. ↗ leaves it or opens a new tab. ← goes back. Arrows are decoration; the words carry the meaning.</td><td class="mono">LinkedIn ↗</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="ui">
    <h2 id="ui">Writing for the interface</h2>
    <h3>Buttons</h3>
    <p class="ds-text">Start with a verb, keep it to one to four words, and say what happens next. The label should still make sense read on its own.</p>
    <DoDont doText="Say what the button does." dontText="Use a label that only makes sense with the sentence around it.">
      <span slot="do" class="btn btn-primary">Send it over</span>
      <span slot="dont" class="btn btn-primary">Submit</span>
    </DoDont>
    <h3>Links</h3>
    <p class="ds-text">Link the words that name the destination. "Read the RHOW Coffee case study," not "click here."</p>
    <h3>Labels and hints</h3>
    <p class="ds-text">
      Labels are short nouns or questions. Hints go between the label and the field, and explain why you're
      asking or what a good answer looks like. Placeholders are for format examples only, never for
      instructions, since they disappear as soon as someone types.
    </p>
    <h3>Errors</h3>
    <p class="ds-text">Say what's wrong and how to fix it, in one sentence, without blame.</p>
    <DoDont doText="Name the fix." dontText="Name the failure.">
      <p slot="do" class="err">Enter an email address, like name@example.com.</p>
      <p slot="dont" class="err">Invalid input.</p>
    </DoDont>
    <h3>Alt text</h3>
    <p class="ds-text">
      Describe what's in the frame, not what it means. Lead with the subject, include any text that matters,
      and skip "image of." If the caption already says it, the alt text should add what the eye sees.
    </p>
    <DoDont
      doText="Brand collection font size tokens from Display 1 to Type XS with separate values for CM, BC, and FDX."
      dontText="Screenshot of tokens."
    />
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="words">
    <h2 id="words">Word list</h2>
    <div class="ds-table-wrap">
      <table class="ds-table">
        <thead><tr><th scope="col">Use</th><th scope="col">Not</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>website</td><td class="muted">web site, site (in headings)</td></tr>
          <tr><td>email</td><td class="muted">e-mail</td></tr>
          <tr><td>design system</td><td class="muted">Design System (mid-sentence)</td></tr>
          <tr><td>case study</td><td class="muted">case-study</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Figma, Webflow, Makeswift, Claude</td><td class="muted">figma, WebFlow</td></tr>
          <tr><td>sign up (verb), sign-up (noun)</td><td class="muted">signup</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="audit">
    <h2 id="audit">Open content issues</h2>
    <p class="ds-text">Found while writing this page. Titles are left for a deliberate pass, since they show in search results.</p>
    <ul class="ds-list">
      <li>Page titles mix two separators: "About — Ryan Sheridan" and "Resume | Ryan Sheridan." Pick one; the pipe avoids the em dash.</li>
      <li>Fixed in 1.0: the About timeline wrote date ranges with an em dash; it now uses an en dash, like the Resume.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>
</DsLayout>

<style>
  .err {
    font-size: var(--text-s);
    color: var(--color-critical);
  }
</style>
